body:
Actually, I need some clarification: it looks to me like `AbstractChannel` actually closes the `closeFuture` _before_ the passed `promise` in the `doClose0` method [here](https://github.com/netty/netty/blob/4.1/transport/src/main/java/io/netty/channel/AbstractChannel.java#L761) so reversing the order of promise completion seems like the right thing to do.

This can be demonstrated with the following test which can be added to `NioSocketChannelTest`:
```java
@Test(timeout = 3000)
    public void testOrderOfClosePromises() throws IOException {
        NioEventLoopGroup group = new NioEventLoopGroup(1);
        ServerSocket socket = new ServerSocket();
        socket.bind(new InetSocketAddress(0));
        Socket accepted = null;
        try {
            Bootstrap sb = new Bootstrap();
            sb.group(group).channel(NioSocketChannel.class);
            sb.handler(new ChannelInboundHandlerAdapter());

            SocketChannel channel = (SocketChannel) sb.connect(socket.getLocalSocketAddress())
                .syncUninterruptibly().channel();

            accepted = socket.accept();

            final AtomicInteger counter = new AtomicInteger(0);

            final AtomicInteger onClosePromiseCount = new AtomicInteger(0);
            final AtomicInteger passedClosePromiseCount = new AtomicInteger(0);

            channel.closeFuture().addListener(new ChannelFutureListener() {
                @Override
                public void operationComplete(ChannelFuture future) throws Exception {
                    onClosePromiseCount.set(counter.getAndIncrement());
                }
            });

            final ChannelPromise f = channel.newPromise();
            f.addListener(new ChannelFutureListener() {
                @Override
                public void operationComplete(ChannelFuture future) throws Exception {
                    passedClosePromiseCount.set(counter.getAndIncrement());
                }
            });

            channel.close(f).syncUninterruptibly();

            assertEquals(0, onClosePromiseCount.get());
            assertEquals(1, passedClosePromiseCount.get());
        } finally {
            if (accepted != null) {
                try {
                    accepted.close();
                } catch (IOException ignore) {
                    // ignore
                }
            }
            try {
                socket.close();
            } catch (IOException ignore) {
                // ignore
            }
            group.shutdownGracefully();
        }
    }
```
with the nugget being the asserts right before the finally block.
